Herpes simplex (Risk Occular)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Grouped vesicles on erythematous base, Mucocutaneous junction location, Prodromal tingling/burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-022
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, painful vesicular eruption near the eye causing significant distress and fear of visual compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly expanding, highly painful cluster of blisters on the upper cheek, perilously close to the lower eyelid. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out Herpes Simplex Keratitis or ophthalmic involvement, which threatens permanent vision loss, and to initiate immediate systemic antiviral therapy within the narrow efficacy window before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My cheek is covered in these burning blisters that popped up overnight, and they are spreading right up to my eye. It hurts to even blink."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 48-hour history of localized prodromal tingling and a severe burning sensation on the right upper cheek, just below the orbital rim. Twelve hours ago, they experienced a rapid eruption of grouped vesicles situated on an erythematous base. The primary location is near the mucocutaneous junction of the lower eyelid. Some vesicles have ruptured, leaving shallow painful ulcerations with early golden crusting. The patient notes mild localized throbbing pain and significant anxiety regarding potential spread into the eye. They report a recent period of intense physiological stress and ultraviolet light exposure (outdoor beach trip) prior to the outbreak.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding vision; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, shielding the right side of the face.
Physical Exam: On the right infraorbital cheek, approximately 1.5 cm from the lower eyelid margin, there is a 2x2 cm cluster of grouped, tense, fluid-filled vesicles on a sharply defined erythematous base. Several vesicles have unroofed, demonstrating shallow, punched-out ulcerations with early serous crusting. Tender regional submandibular lymphadenopathy is palpable on the right. Crucially, visual acuity is grossly intact. There is no conjunctival injection, no corneal clouding, no dendritic ulcers visible on gross inspection, and extraocular movements are painless and normal.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications. No history of immunosuppression.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cutaneous Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) Infection - Status: Acute outbreak
Differentials:

Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesion is highly localized, crosses no distinct dermatomal lines of the V1 distribution (it is on the cheek/V2 area), and lacks the severe, deep neuralgic pain typical of zoster.

Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic prodromal burning/tingling, the presence of intact tense vesicles rather than primary honey-colored crusts, and the rapid overnight onset.

Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly unilateral, grouped vesicular nature of the rash on an erythematous base, coupled with the classic neuropathic prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute cutaneous HSV eruption in high-risk proximity to the orbit. Triage accurately flagged the rapid spread of vesicles toward the eye as a potential vision-threatening emergency requiring immediate evaluation. Urgent out-of-hours clinical assessment was mandated to perform a basic ophthalmic exam, ruling out direct corneal or conjunctival involvement, which would require emergent ophthalmology transfer. Confirming the infection remained purely cutaneous allowed for immediate prescription of high-dose oral antivirals to arrest viral replication and halt the proximal spread, safely managing the patient at home and preventing severe complications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Valacyclovir 2000mg stat, followed by 2000mg 12 hours later (episodic high-dose therapy) to rapidly halt viral replication. Advised simple analgesia (Ibuprofen 400mg) for the burning pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking this high-dose antiviral medication exactly as prescribed today, you will rapidly shut down the virus's ability to multiply, stopping the blisters from spreading closer to your eye. By strictly washing your hands with soap and water every single time you accidentally touch your face, you will prevent physically transferring the virus into your eye or onto other people.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow to reassess the lesion's proximity to the eye and confirm no delayed ocular symptoms have developed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop a red, bloodshot eye, experience pain when looking at bright lights (photophobia), feel like there is sand in your eye, or notice any blurring of your vision, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While the eye is currently clear, the virus is highly contagious and can be spread to the eye via your fingers.
Clinical Course: The blisters will likely rupture, crust over, and heal completely within 7 to 10 days. The antiviral medication will speed this process and reduce the pain.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Strict hand hygiene is mandatory. Seek emergency medical care immediately for any changes in vision or onset of eye pain.
